                <h2>Correct the Review Framework: Delivery Does Not Equal Sales</h2>
<p>A Meta creative that generates spend and clicks is only capable of basic delivery. To determine whether it can actually sell, review three additional questions:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Is the traffic relevant?</strong> Does the audience match the product use case, and where are clicks coming from across placements, locations, and time periods?</li>
<li><strong>Are the conversions genuine?</strong> Can the landing page fulfill the ad promise and support a complete journey from add to cart or registration to purchase?</li>
<li><strong>Are the sales profitable?</strong> After product cost, payment fees, logistics, commissions, and refunds are deducted, does the campaign still generate positive contribution margin?</li>
</ul>
<p>Do not judge a creative by CTR alone, and do not treat platform-attributed purchases as profit. A video ad for a utility app may attract strong clicks while producing weak new-user retention. Likewise, a cross-border e-commerce team may have a stable image ad that repeatedly stops because its landing page is out of stock. Both cases show that delivery is only the first gate.</p>
<h2>A 30-Minute Creative Review Checklist</h2>
<h3>1. Review Performance by Traffic, Conversion, and Profit</h3>
<p>Open Meta Ads Manager and create one record for every creative with the following fields:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Traffic:</strong> impressions, frequency, CTR, and cost per 1,000 impressions.</li>
<li><strong>Conversion:</strong> landing-page views, add-to-carts, registrations, checkout starts, and purchases.</li>
<li><strong>Business results:</strong> average order value, refund rate, settled revenue, and cost per retained sale.</li>
</ul>
<p>If CTR is high but landing-page-view rate is low, check page speed, redirect errors, and regional compatibility. If add-to-carts are healthy but purchases are weak, inspect shipping costs, payment options, inventory, and checkout usability. If orders are frequent but margins are negative, ROAS alone cannot justify further scaling.</p>
<h3>2. Assign Each Creative One of Four Statuses</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Promising but unproven:</strong> clicks and add-to-carts are promising, but the sample is too small; continue with a controlled test budget.</li>
<li><strong>High clicks, weak conversion:</strong> the creative may overpromise or the landing page may not match; update both before testing again.</li>
<li><strong>Low clicks, strong conversion:</strong> the offer or page may work; test clearer value propositions and a stronger first frame.</li>
<li><strong>High spend, weak profit:</strong> reduce the budget and review bids, discounts, locations, and audience quality.</li>
</ul>
<p>Keep the original creative ID, revision history, and launch date. Otherwise, a team may relaunch what it believes is a new version while actually retesting the same asset.</p>
<h3>3. Determine Whether Audience Settings Are Amplifying the Problem</h3>
<p>Split results by new and returning customers, broad interests, and remarketing audiences. Review whether spend is concentrated in low-quality locations or placements, whether conversion rate declines as frequency rises, whether exclusions are too narrow, and whether automatic placements introduce irrelevant traffic.</p>
<p>Test creative and audience issues separately. Otherwise, poor audience targeting may be mistaken for weak creative, or a narrow high-converting audience may hide a weak ad.</p>
<h3>4. Audit the Post-Click Journey</h3>
<p>Click through each priority creative and check whether the landing-page headline immediately explains the product, its use case, and the next action. Verify mobile loading, forms, payments, inventory, discount codes, and redirects.</p>
<p>Ask a colleague to complete an order using a different device, operating system, and connection. This can reveal checkout friction that is hidden when the media buyer repeatedly tests the same setup.</p>
<h3>5. Reconcile the Final Result Using Finance Data</h3>
<p>Compare Meta reporting with payment records, the order system, and funding transactions. Attribution delays mean operational reports should distinguish platform-attributed purchases from settled revenue.</p>
<p>Use a consistent record:</p>
<p><code>Creative ID | Ad account | Audience | Spend | Clicks | Add-to-carts | Platform purchases | Settled revenue | Refunds | Product cost | Logistics and commissions | Cost per retained sale</code></p>
<p>For account funding, also reconcile available balance, recorded spend, and billing charges. Set balance alerts and maintain sufficient funds so a strong creative is not interrupted by a funding shortage.</p>
<h2>Final Decision: Scale, Restrict, or Rebuild</h2>
<p>At the end of each weekly review, assign every creative one of three actions: scale, restrict the budget, or pause and rebuild. Before scaling, confirm that the conversion path is stable, the account has sufficient balance, and the target audience is not trapped in an ineffective traffic pool. When pausing a creative, document whether the issue is weak engagement, weak conversion, negative margin, or a landing-page fault.</p>
<p>A more useful decision formula is:</p>
<p><strong>Creative value = retained transactions × actual gross margin − advertising and fulfillment costs</strong></p>
<p>Calculate this figure before deciding whether a creative should continue running. This turns the review from a report about which ads spent the most into a clear assessment of which creatives help the business sell products and preserve profit.</p>
